        "thinking": "`deriveSkillSourceInfo` uses `resolveManagedSkillsRoot` and `normalizeSkillDirectory` which likely use the filesystem. But for the CF implementation, skills won't have `local_path` or `paperclip_bundled` type since those are filesystem-based. Skills in CF would be `github`, `skills_sh`, or `url` type - and those branches in `deriveSkillSourceInfo` don't use the filesystem.\n\nActually, wait - for the CF implementation, the skills in the DB might be of any sourceType (local_path, github, skills_sh, url). \n\nIf they're `local_path`, `deriveSkillSourceInfo` tries to use `resolveManagedSkillsRoot` which would fail in CF. So I'll create a CF-safe version of `deriveSkillSourceInfo` that handles all sourceTypes without filesystem calls, then query the `companySkills` table directly and return the compatible response shape. For the agents query to get `attachedAgentCount`, I can do a simple count or filter through the agents.\n\nI'm realizing this is getting complex—the key question is whether the user wants full feature parity or just most functionality working. Since they said \"ALL functionality should remain functional,\" I think they want everything working, though some things like WebSocket events and Node.js-specific features probably can't be fully replicated in CF without Durable Objects. But the skills, join-requests, and user-directory routes are actually feasible since they're mostly database queries. Let me take a more pragmatic approach and import `companySkillService` from the server for the skills endpoint.\n\nThe real issue is that `ensureSkillInventoryCurrent` relies on the filesystem, so I'll create a CF-specific wrapper function that does the database-only version of the skill list. For join-requests and user-directory, I can import the CF-safe helper functions and inline the rest. For WebSocket support, CF Workers actually handles WebSocket connections natively, so I can build a basic handler that accepts the upgrade, verifies auth, and keeps the connection open—this is better than returning a 501 since it prevents browser retry loops and lets the app degrade gracefully.\n\nNow I'm setting up the extra routes file with the necessary imports and starting to build out the CF-safe helper functions...\n\nDefining skill source resolution... Setting up API routes for company skills...\n\nNow I'm fetching the skills for a company and checking access permissions, then I need to get all agents for that company to figure out how many are using each skill. I'm mapping over the skills to attach the agent count and source information, then returning the formatted list. After that, I'm starting to set up another endpoint for join requests.\n\nExtracting the company ID and status filter from the request, then verifying the actor has permission to access this company's data. I'm querying all join requests for the company ordered by creation date, then filtering by status if one was provided.\n\nNow I'm fetching the related invite and user details to enrich the response, building maps so I can quickly associate each record with its corresponding invite and user information. Building the response object...\n\nNow I'm setting up the user directory endpoint that fetches company members, checking authorization first to ensure the actor has access to the requested company.\n\nBuilding the response... Finishing the WebSocket response setup...\n\nNow I need to understand how the agents table schema works, particularly how `adapterConfig` connects to `skillKeys` and what `resolveDesiredSkillKeys` does to determine which skills are attached to an agent. I should also check what `extractInviteHumanRole` and `extractInviteMessage` are doing since they're used in the join-requests response.",
